ReLoop AI: Giving Every Discarded Device a Second Life

Capstone Project Proposal – Initial Submission for Approval



1. Project Title

ReLoop AI: From E-Waste to Second Life. An AI-Guided Decision System for the Repair, Reuse and Recycling of Electronic Devices

2. Abstract

Millions of tonnes of electronics are thrown away every year, and much of it is not really waste: a phone with a tired battery, a charger with a worn cable, earbuds that need a small fix. People discard these items out of uncertainty, not indifference. They cannot tell whether a device can be repaired, resold or must be recycled safely, and they do not know where to take it. ReLoop AI answers that question in minutes. A user photographs an unwanted device; a lightweight image classifier identifies it, a short condition check captures its age and state, and an explainable decision engine recommends the best pathway: repair, refurbish and resell, harvest for parts, or safe recycling, with strict safety rules for damaged batteries. The app then connects the user to verified repair shops and authorised collection points nearby. We hypothesise that this end-to-end guidance will choose the right pathway more often, and reach a suitable option faster, than image-only identification or manual search. Results will be reported using pathway accuracy, classification metrics, task time and an estimated environmental benefit.

3. Introduction

Background. The world generated an estimated 62 million tonnes of e-waste in 2022, and only about a fifth of it was documented as properly collected and recycled (Global E-waste Monitor 2024). India is among the largest contributors, and the E-Waste (Management) Rules, 2022 now make producers responsible for what happens to their products at end of life.

Importance. Every discarded gadget is a mix of treasure and trouble. Copper, gold, cobalt and rare earths are worth recovering. Lead, mercury and flame retardants are dangerous when burned or dissolved in informal recycling. A damaged lithium-ion battery can start a fire in a bin or a collection truck. Meanwhile, plenty of devices that could have been repaired or reused are lost along the way.

Motivation. Most people do not throw electronics away because they do not care; they do it because they do not know. Can this be fixed? Is it worth anything? Is it dangerous? Where do I take it? ReLoop AI exists to answer those four questions in one place. We chose this problem because it is real, relatable and measurable: it involves phones, chargers, batteries and appliances that every student owns, and it can be built and tested as a focused prototype.

4. Problem Statement

Someone holding an unwanted electronic item has no simple way to find out whether it can be repaired, refurbished, stripped for parts or must be recycled, or who will actually accept it. So items get binned, stockpiled in drawers or handed to informal collectors.

This deserves a real solution because wrong disposal harms people and the environment, reusable value and materials are lost, repair and recycling information is scattered and hard to trust, and a tool that ends in a concrete next step changes behaviour in a way that awareness campaigns alone rarely do.

