6. Proposed System

ReLoop AI works like a friendly triage desk for old electronics: one photo, four steps, one clear answer.

1.Identify. Snap a photo. A fine-tuned lightweight CNN predicts the item category (phone, charger, power bank, earbuds, laptop, small appliance) with a confidence score. If it is unsure, it asks for another photo instead of guessing.

2.Assess. A short adaptive questionnaire captures age, brand or model (optional), working status, visible damage and battery condition such as swelling or overheating.

3.Decide. A transparent rules-based engine scores each pathway (repair, refurbish and resell, harvest components, recycle, hazardous handling) and returns a ranked recommendation with reasons and any safety warning.

4.Connect. The result is matched to a curated directory of verified repair shops, refurbishers and collection points, filtered by distance and accepted item types.

How it addresses the limitations:

From "what is it?" to "what now?" Identification is linked directly to an action, instead of stopping at a label.

Condition-aware. Age, damage and battery state shape the advice, which image-only tools ignore.

Explainable. Every recommendation comes with reasons, so it can be audited rather than trusted blindly.

Honest about uncertainty. It asks for more input instead of guessing, and safety always comes first for damaged batteries.

Local and verified. It relies on a verified, locally configurable directory rather than generic advice.

7. Project Hypothesis

For common consumer electronic items, ReLoop AI's combined image-plus-condition decision engine will recommend the expert-validated pathway more accurately than image-only classification mapped to a fixed category rule, and will let users reach an appropriate repair, reuse or recycling option faster than manual web search.

Expected improvement and evaluation:

Pathway accuracy is expected to exceed the image-only baseline by a clear margin (target: at least 15 percentage points).

Median time to reach a suitable partner is expected to fall by at least 40% compared with manual search.

Both will be tested on a held-out set of scenarios labelled by domain experts and in a small user task study (see Section 13). Targets are initial goals and will be refined after a pilot.

8. Objectives

1.Identify: fine-tune an image classifier for at least 8 consumer e-waste categories, reaching a macro-F1 of at least 0.85 on a held-out test set.

2.Decide: build an explainable rules engine with five pathways that agrees with expert-labelled scenarios at least 80% of the time.

3.Connect: curate a verified directory of at least 30 repair, refurbishment and collection partners in one pilot city.

4.Deliver: ship a working, mobile-friendly prototype of the full identify, assess, decide and connect flow.

5.Prove: evaluate against baselines with at least 15 participants, reporting pathway accuracy, time to decision and estimated environmental benefit.

9. Proposed Methodology

Step 1: Requirement study. Review the E-Waste (Management) Rules, collection-point and recycler lists, and repairability resources. Interview 10 to 15 potential users (students, households, hostel and campus staff) and 3 to 5 repair or recycling operators to define item categories, decision criteria and pain points.

Step 2: Data collection and preparation. Assemble the image dataset and the partner directory (Section 10).

Step 3: Image identification module. Fine-tune a pretrained MobileNetV2 (primary) and EfficientNet-B0 (comparison) using transfer learning, data augmentation (rotation, brightness, crop, blur) and class-weighted loss. Apply a confidence threshold; below it the system requests another photo or manual selection. Optional: Grad-CAM for interpretability.

Step 4: Decision engine. Encode expert knowledge as weighted rules. Inputs: category, age, working status, damage, battery condition, brand support. Each pathway receives a score; the highest valid score is recommended, subject to hard safety overrides (for example, a swollen battery always routes to safe hazardous handling and is never offered for resale or DIY repair). The output includes reasons, an estimated residual value band and a repair feasibility rating based on category-level repairability heuristics.

Step 5: Partner matching. Filter the directory by pathway, item type and distance (haversine), rank by distance and verification status.

Step 6: Impact estimator. Convert the recommended pathway into an indicative saving (kg of e-waste diverted, approximate CO2e avoided) using published average emission factors, always shown as an estimate.

Step 7: Integration and UI. Build the backend API and a responsive web interface with an optional camera input.

Step 8: Testing and evaluation. Run the evaluation plan (Section 13), iterate and document.

